1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Antibodies are good at grabbing viruses outside cells.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,360
But a virus doesn't stay

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:06,453
outside.

4
00:00:06,507 --> 00:00:08,320
It gets into your own cells and copies

5
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:11,733
itself there, where antibodies can't reach it.

6
00:00:11,867 --> 00:00:13,760
So how does your body find the infected

7
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:16,440
cells among billions of healthy ones?

8
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:20,080
In 1973, in Canberra, Peter Doherty and Rolf

9
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:22,960
Zinkernagel helped answer that, and later shared a

10
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,613
Nobel Prize for it.

11
00:00:24,667 --> 00:00:26,880
The answer involves T cells.

12
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:29,860
Here's our case.

13
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:32,180
A virus lands on the lining of your

14
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:33,140
airways.

15
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:35,940
A protein on its surface fits a molecule

16
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:38,740
on an airway cell, so it binds and

17
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:39,380
gets in.

18
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:42,420
Inside, it takes over the cell and uses

19
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:44,500
it to make many new viruses.

20
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:47,580
Those leave and infect the cells next door.

21
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:50,580
While the virus is inside a cell, antibodies

22
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:51,620
can't touch it.

23
00:00:53,634 --> 00:00:56,313
So the body checks cells from the outside.

24
00:00:56,594 --> 00:00:58,993
Almost every one of your cells cuts up

25
00:00:59,074 --> 00:01:01,473
some of the proteins it's making, and displays

26
00:01:01,634 --> 00:01:04,273
small pieces on its surface, held in display

27
00:01:04,354 --> 00:01:05,313
molecules.

28
00:01:05,634 --> 00:01:07,873
A healthy cell displays pieces of its own

29
00:01:07,954 --> 00:01:08,673
proteins.

30
00:01:09,154 --> 00:01:12,513
An infected cell is making virus proteins too,

31
00:01:12,594 --> 00:01:15,233
so it displays virus pieces as well.

32
00:01:15,554 --> 00:01:19,073
Showing pieces like this is called antigen presentation.

33
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:24,100
T cells are lymphocytes, like B cells.

34
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:27,020
Each T cell has receptors of one shape,

35
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,180
made at random as it developed.

36
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:30,500
But here's the difference.

37
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:33,340
A T cell receptor doesn't bind a free

38
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:34,380
virus.

39
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,540
It only fits a piece that's displayed on

40
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:39,580
a cell, held in a display molecule.

41
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:42,700
Doherty and Zinkernagel found the clue: a T

42
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:45,420
cell recognises a virus only together with the

43
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:47,740
cell's own display molecule.

44
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:49,740
So it checks the display molecule and the

45
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:51,100
piece together.

46
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:55,340
First, though, the right T cells have to

47
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:56,340
be found.

48
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:59,500
In the last two videos, dendritic cells carried

49
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:01,500
pieces of the pathogen to a lymph node

50
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:02,900
and displayed them.

51
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:05,740
Only the few T cells whose receptors fit

52
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:07,340
those pieces are selected.

53
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:10,460
They divide into clones over several days, just

54
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:11,580
as B cells do.

55
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:14,700
And the clones are of two main kinds,

56
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:17,820
helper T cells and cytotoxic T cells.

57
00:02:19,834 --> 00:02:22,153
Helper T cells mainly coordinate.

58
00:02:22,554 --> 00:02:25,193
They send signals that help other cells act.

59
00:02:25,754 --> 00:02:27,593
When a helper T cell finds the same

60
00:02:27,754 --> 00:02:29,753
pieces displayed on a B cell that has

61
00:02:29,914 --> 00:02:32,873
taken in the antigen, it releases signal chemicals

62
00:02:32,954 --> 00:02:34,393
called cytokines.

63
00:02:34,634 --> 00:02:37,033
That's the go-ahead signal from the last video.

64
00:02:37,354 --> 00:02:40,153
Helper T cells also release cytokines that make

65
00:02:40,234 --> 00:02:44,473
cytotoxic T cells multiply, and make macrophages better

66
00:02:44,554 --> 00:02:46,313
at destroying what they engulf.

67
00:02:48,334 --> 00:02:51,133
Cytotoxic T cells are the killers.

68
00:02:51,374 --> 00:02:53,253
They leave the lymph node, travel in the

69
00:02:53,294 --> 00:02:56,653
blood, and squeeze out into the infected airway.

70
00:02:56,974 --> 00:02:59,533
There, they check the pieces displayed on each

71
00:02:59,614 --> 00:03:00,013
cell.

72
00:03:00,574 --> 00:03:03,613
Healthy cells, displaying only their own pieces, are

73
00:03:03,694 --> 00:03:04,613
left alone.

74
00:03:04,974 --> 00:03:07,533
When a cytotoxic T cell's receptor fits a

75
00:03:07,614 --> 00:03:10,653
virus piece on an infected cell, it binds

76
00:03:10,694 --> 00:03:11,533
to that cell.

77
00:03:13,534 --> 00:03:15,213
Then it releases proteins.

78
00:03:15,774 --> 00:03:18,573
Some make holes in the infected cell's membrane.

79
00:03:18,814 --> 00:03:21,293
Others pass through and switch on the cell's

80
00:03:21,374 --> 00:03:24,733
own self-destruct program, called apoptosis.

81
00:03:24,974 --> 00:03:27,053
The cell breaks itself up in a tidy

82
00:03:27,214 --> 00:03:29,453
way, and phagocytes clear the pieces.

83
00:03:29,934 --> 00:03:32,493
Virus copies still being built inside are lost

84
00:03:32,574 --> 00:03:35,453
with it, so the virus loses its factory.

85
00:03:35,694 --> 00:03:37,293
The T cell then moves on to the

86
00:03:37,454 --> 00:03:38,733
next infected cell.

87
00:03:40,734 --> 00:03:43,613
Cytotoxic T cells aren't the only cells that

88
00:03:43,774 --> 00:03:45,253
kill infected cells.

89
00:03:45,534 --> 00:03:48,973
Natural killer cells, or NK cells, are part

90
00:03:49,054 --> 00:03:50,093
of the innate system.

91
00:03:50,574 --> 00:03:53,293
They don't have receptors made for one pathogen.

92
00:03:53,534 --> 00:03:55,773
They respond to stress signals on a cell,

93
00:03:55,854 --> 00:03:59,213
and to cells displaying too few display molecules,

94
00:03:59,294 --> 00:04:01,293
which is one way some viruses hide from

95
00:04:01,374 --> 00:04:02,093
T cells.

96
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:04,493
NK cells act early.

97
00:04:04,974 --> 00:04:08,173
Cytotoxic T cells take longer, but they target

98
00:04:08,334 --> 00:04:09,293
one pathogen.

99
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,780
So the adaptive response has two arms.

100
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:17,300
In the antibody arm, B cells make antibodies

101
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:20,420
that act on pathogens outside cells, in blood

102
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:21,460
and tissue fluid.

103
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:25,620
In the cell-mediated arm, cytotoxic T cells destroy

104
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:26,660
infected cells.

105
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:29,780
You need both, because a virus is outside

106
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:32,580
cells while it travels between them, and inside

107
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,180
cells while it copies itself.

108
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:36,900
Helper T cells sit in the middle, switching

109
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:37,620
on both.

110
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:41,220
Some T cells also become memory T cells,

111
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:43,700
and the next video shows why that matters.

112
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:47,540
Here's a way to keep them apart.

113
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:50,100
B cells make antiBodies.

114
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:53,060
T cells Terminate or Tell.

115
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,740
Cytotoxic T cells terminate infected cells.

116
00:04:56,980 --> 00:04:59,700
Helper T cells tell other cells to act.

117
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:03,220
Two exam questions now.

118
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:04,260
First.

119
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:07,540
Cytotoxic T cells were taken from a mouse

120
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:09,380
infected with virus X.

121
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:12,260
They were mixed with three groups of cells,

122
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:14,180
all from mice of the same strain.

123
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:17,220
Group one cells were uninfected.

124
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:20,420
Group two cells were infected with virus X.

125
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:24,020
Group three cells were infected with virus Y.

126
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:26,900
Only the group two cells were killed.

127
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:28,980
Explain these results.

128
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:30,580
Pause here to answer.

129
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:45,560
Virus X selected cytotoxic T cells whose receptors

130
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,160
fit X's pieces.

131
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:50,600
Group two cells displayed virus X pieces, so

132
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:53,680
the T cells bound them and triggered apoptosis.

133
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:57,480
Group one cells displayed only their own pieces.

134
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:00,680
Group three displayed virus Y pieces, which didn't

135
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:02,040
fit these receptors.

136
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:03,720
So neither was killed.

137
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:06,200
Saying the T cells attacked the virus itself

138
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:07,800
is the classic mistake.

139
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:10,280
Second.

140
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:14,680
HIV infects and destroys helper T cells.

141
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,880
Without treatment, their number in the blood falls

142
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,360
over several years.

143
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:22,520
A student says, this weakens only the T

144
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:25,400
cell arm of the immune system, because antibodies

145
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:27,080
are made by B cells.

146
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:29,240
Evaluate the student's claim.

147
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:30,760
Pause here to answer.

148
00:06:41,967 --> 00:06:44,766
B cells do make antibodies, but the claim

149
00:06:44,847 --> 00:06:46,286
isn't supported.

150
00:06:46,447 --> 00:06:48,606
Most B cells need a helper T cell's

151
00:06:48,687 --> 00:06:52,206
go-ahead before they multiply into plasma cells, so

152
00:06:52,327 --> 00:06:55,006
antibody responses weaken too.

153
00:06:55,047 --> 00:06:58,046
Cytotoxic T cells rely on helper cytokines to

154
00:06:58,327 --> 00:07:01,166
multiply, so killing of infected cells weakens as

155
00:07:01,247 --> 00:07:02,046
well.

156
00:07:02,207 --> 00:07:04,286
Both arms weaken together.

157
00:07:04,447 --> 00:07:06,846
Thinking B cells work without helper T cells

158
00:07:06,927 --> 00:07:08,446
is the classic mistake.

159
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:13,586
So for any T cell question, say which

160
00:07:13,667 --> 00:07:16,546
cell displays the antigen, which T cells fit

161
00:07:16,627 --> 00:07:19,986
it and multiply, what helper T cells signal,

162
00:07:20,067 --> 00:07:24,146
and how cytotoxic T cells destroy infected cells.

163
00:07:24,707 --> 00:07:27,426
Next, why a second infection is often stopped

164
00:07:27,667 --> 00:07:28,466
much faster.

165
00:07:30,467 --> 00:07:31,906
Here are the key notes.

166
00:07:32,147 --> 00:07:34,066
Pause the video if you need more time

167
00:07:34,147 --> 00:07:34,946
to copy them.
